From: tburns@gmuvax.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Subject: Rights and Concepts

At  4:13 PM 9/13/93 -0500, Freeman Craig Presson wrote:
>Carl Menger wrote a whole book
>|> on it: "On the Origin of Money." 

This was an article. There's a chapter on money in his "Principles of Economics"
that supplies the essentials of his view.

"With economic progress, therefore, we can everywhere observe the
phenomenon of a certain number of goods, especially those that are most
easily saleable at a given time and place, becoming under the powerful
influence of custom, acceptable to everyone in trade, and thus capable of
being given in exchange for any other commodity." (Menger, *Principles of
Economics*, page 260, 1976 edition published by IHS.)

Barter where both parties get the good they really want is nearly
impossible, so persons will make indirect trades, trading what they have
for something more more tradeable, then take that to persons/places they
think they can get what they want. Eventually people will figure out what
is most acceptable to everyone else and they all converge on a set of money
commodities. One or more of these commodities will be involved in almost
all trades. Coinage, banking, etc. builds on that basic idea of a common
medium of exchange.

>Anyone have a thumbnail review of Menger on Money?

>
>* easily transferred (which usually but not always means portable) an
>  indefinite number of times at minimal cost.
>* has one unit, so can be added/subtracted. (It arithmetizes wealth)
>* stands for specific (possibly variable) amounts of goods.
>And, in what may have been a later development,
>* It can be exchanged for services as well as tangible goods.

These are not always satisfied by primitive money. Examples: cocoa beans,
goose quills, tobacco and dried fish. We could quibble about whether these
were money. The point is, if you were a farmer, this is the stuff you
traded your crops for (generally). And when you went to town to buy
something, you took some of this along because you expected any person
possessing what you want to accept some amount of this stuff in exchange
(without knowing the individual preferences of the person you were trading
with).

