Very good example: tobacco in colonial Virginia, court records are
full of instances of barrels of 'tobo.' being use to settle debts,
etc. Ultimately this was one of the money substitutes that didn't fly,
since the medium of exchange had this nasty tendedncy to rot if kept
too long.

Another example of this sort: whiskey in the midatlantic/midsouth
states until 1794. This worked better because it aged in the barrel
instead of rotting. The excise tax and the rebellion of 1794 killed it
for irrelevant reasons. It's no wonder a 25% excise tax[1] caused a
rebellion when it was levied on the main medium of exchange.

The main early argument for a central bank was that although there
was a defined currency, the _supply_ of it was spotty, and farmers
could not always be paid for their crops in recognized coin.

