From: "Perry E. Metzger" <unccsun.uncc.edu!lehman.com!pmetzger>

PM> Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering says:
PM> [Argues against my "Brain in vat" scenario on semantic grounds]

Where was his argument based on semantics? Or do you just mean that 
you didn't understand it?

PM> Elias, this has very little to do with Ayn Rand's problem about denial 
PM> of the evidence of the senses -- I suggest learning about the 
PM> objectivist position on the subject and then checking whether my 
PM> counterargument is sufficient or not.

This is really quite amusing. After reading Elias's posts on 
objectivism for several years, I consider him to have perhaps the best 
understanding of objectivism of anyone on the net.

PM> Summarized, they argue that what you perceive must be THE reality.

The expression "THE reality" makes no sense. Reality is whatever 
exists, perceived or not, perceptable or not. 

The objection to the vatted-brain/VR scenario is actually much 
stronger than I have yet seen presented; however, I don't expect Perry
to accept it since he rejects the two foundational premises: 1) there 
is one and only one reality (ie, existence exists) and 2) 
contradictions cannot exist (ie, A is A).

The essential point of the argument is that the vatted-brain/VR would 
have to exist in "the real world"; and to ensure absolutely no 
discrepancies (ie, contradictions) between the VR and the "real world" 
would require omniscience and omnipotence. Of course if the VR 
perfectly conveyed information about the real world then it would 
simply be the vatted-brain's sensory apparatus. (And yes, our senses 
perfectly convey information about the real world. It is our 
judgements about our perceptions that are often faulty.)

From: unccsun.uncc.edu!cs.wisc.edu!derek (Derek Zahn)

DZ> I haven't been following it too closely -- but if somebody is really 
DZ> claiming that our senses reveal an objective reality unerringly, 
DZ> that's clearly hooey.

I don't think that claim has yet been specifcally made in this thread,
but it is not hooey.

DZ> When two witnesses of some event see that event differently (as 
DZ> apparently happens all the time), wouldn't that be a contradiction?

Of course not. The two witnesses each perceive the event from 
different perspectives. Even if somehow the event affected their 
senses identically there is no guaranty that their judgements 
resulting therefrom would be identical.

From: unccsun.uncc.edu!jido.b30.ingr.com!extr (Freeman Craig Presson)

FCP> The difference makes a difference when the percept fails to match
FCP> reality, which happens all the time; the difference is often the
FCP> injury or death of the person.

How can the percept "fail to match reality"? What would that even 
mean? Perception's an entirely mechanical process. Certainly our 
judgement of our perception can err but not the perception itself.

From: "Perry E. Metzger" <unccsun.uncc.edu!lehman.com!pmetzger>

PM> There are obviously two realities in the case we are discussing
PM> -- the Randroids would argue that this is impossible.

Really? Isn't one of them just some sensory inputs? What is your 
definition of "reality"?

