Point 2. is the crux, IMO.  Point 1. is a red herring (after all,
nanotech is just a score or two orders of magnitude higher - doesn't
take long for an exponential combinatorial explosion to blow up, no
matter how advanced the tech).

> (My apologies to Mike for the indignant flame.)

No sweat.  Nice to know some people are persuaded by argument. A pity
some others (like Tim Starr) can't do likewise.

> Still, I have a nagging feeling that a simulated reality would seem
> "canned" to the subject [various ways of checking the consistency of
> the "reality" suggested] My point: That's gotta be one *hell* of a 
> computer, with one *hell* of a hard drive.

It should be easy to produce a convincing simulation, with a high
degree of internal consistency.  Suppose the subject starts checking
things out, trying to trip up the simulator.  Slow down the subject's
hardware clock by a million or more as crucial points are reached and
more fabrication of consistent lies are required (eg as the subject
examines a table through a lens (as in Perry's example).  That way the
simulator need not be super-duper fast to out think the subject.

Should the subject actually find an error - ie experience input 
inconsistent with its memories - then it's still not the end of its
world (!).  Just retroactively adjust its memories.  If I were paranoid
I could believe this happens all the time.  How many times have you
mislaid something and not remembered where you put it until *after*
you've found it?  Happens to me all the time.

