Date: Tue, 14 Sep 93 0:45:42 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: The Landfill is Our Friend

Several folks have commented on landfills: the mining of metals and
even information from them.


Sasha writes:

>    I would expect that an appropriate combination of nanotech, AI and data
> storage technologies to info-mine the landfills may be available in about
> 50 years from now.
>  
>    Don't sell your landfill cheap!

A few years ago I did some illuminating calculations. Someone was
claiming that California, the U.S., the world, etc. is "running out of
landfill space." I had doubts, so in the best hyperrationalist
tradition, I got out my H-P 48sx calculator and did some calculations.

Personal household garbage is an area we can start analyzing
easily--corporate garbage is harder to get figures for, though I'm
sure they exist. For now, let's look at household garbage, which
probably makes up about half of all garbage.

The average family apparently produces about 2 cans of garbage at
curbside per week, based on my observations of what's left at
curbside. About one can per person per week is a fair enough estimate,
within a small factor of the more precise figure. 

A full can is about 50 pounds (most carriers have weight limits in
this area). Call it 25 kilos a week per person. (A bit more if glass
is not recycled, if newspapers are included, etc.)

For 250 million Americans, this is 6 x 10^9 kilos per week in the
nation, or 3 x 10^11 kilos per year. Whew!

(By the way, the per person yearly amount of garbage is about 1300
kilos, or 3000 pounds, or 1.5 tons. About the weight of a car.
Remember this.)

But how much *volume* is this? Garbage is quickly crushed to a density
about that of water (some heavier stuff, like cans, and some lighter
stuff, like paper, etc.). And after being buried under a heap of
garbage, the density goes up further...but this is a minor factor, so
I'll just leave it at water density.

A cubic meter of water masses at 1000 kilos, so the volume of
household garbage is about 3 x 10^8 cubic meters--produced in all of
America every year. 

This would form a cube 670 meters on a side! But if dumped into region
10 kilometers on a side, it would only be 3 meters high! Barely
noticeable on a geological scale.

The Meteor Crater near Winslow, Arizona, is, by sheer coincidence,
about 3 x 10^8 cubic meters, which means it could hold the nation's
household garbage for one year. Not so impressive until one realizes
just how small this crater is (1200 meters across, 200 meters deep)
and how much open desert is around it. (I'm not suggesting we dig such
craters, just making the point about how much room is out there.)

Large copper and coal mines could also hold a lot. (Copper and lead
are toxic, so backfilling them with garbage shouldn't be much worse
than what they started as, lest anyone freak out about "disturbing the
natural balance.")

There is no reason, ipso facto, to concentrate all the garbage in one
giant heap, sort of a "National Midden." It may make a lot more sense
to have 100, or 1000 or more of them. The point is, the entire trash
output of the U.S. is insignificant on a size scale of natural
features...valleys, dry lake beds, etc.

But, critics argue, garbage must be disposed of locally. Why?

Consider the costs of transporting the garbage. There are several easy
ways to look at this. Remember that each person generates the weight
of a car in garbage per year. Car manufacturers routinely ship
3000-pound cars over the distance we're talking about for a few
hundred dollars (so say the shipping invoices). This suggests the
maximum cost to ship all the nation's garbage to one central site
would be on the order of $200 per person per year, or about $15 per
person per month. This is more than my monthly bill is now, but not by
much.

And it should be cheaper, actually. First, the actual sites will
likely be much closer. Not in the local neighborhood (a common fallacy
is that Islip, Long Island, for example, should solve "its" garbage
problem locally). Shipping distance could be 5x lower, on average,
than car shippers deal with.

Second, garbage is non time-critical, isn't easily damaged in transit,
and so can use the absolute cheapest shipping methods. Recall the
Islip garbage barge. Trains, barges, even airships could haul the
garbage. (Barges can't make it into the barren regions my proposal
favors, unless it's up the Columbia River...)

I would guess the shipping costs could be brought down to about $100
per person per year, or $8 per person per month. Plus the local fees
now being paid, less the "dump surcharges" for storing the garbage
locally. 

Naturally, I favor a free market solution, with enterprising owners of
large tracts of land--the American Indian tribes would appear to be
uniquely well-positioned to exploit this approach--offering to accept
large amounts of trash. Isolated desert valleys could be filled with
decades worth of garbage, then covered with dirt and sand, and the
effect would be nearly invisible.

The key point is that we're *not* "running out of disposal space." Not
even close.


Part II -- Why Landfills Are *Good* for the Environment

* Landfills are usually anaerobic, i.e., bacterial and chemical
decomposition proceeds slowly, or is halted. (The media loves to
report horror stories about how landfills have readable newspapers
from 1910...this is cited as a failure of landfills to properly
"recycle.")

* But decomposition is what we want to *avoid*, as that adds to the
CO2 budget of the atmosphere, something which some scientists say is a
bad thing (I'm not taking a stand on this, just noting the
conventional wisdom about the Greenhouse Effect.)

* Letting a tree grow (consuming CO2 and generating oxygen) and then
burying the paper products so that the cycle is broken, for at least
hundred of years and perhaps longer, would seem to be helping the
Greenhouse Effect. 

So, don't recycle those newspapers--bury them! And don't burn them (use nukes
instead, of course).

There's plenty of space for all the garbage we'll ever produce, and
the middens will help avoid the Greenhouse Effect.


-Tim May


