Subject: The Evidence of the Senses 

> From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>

> but saying "I don't like the fact that my senses
> might have nothing to do with an objective reality, so I'm going to
> yell a lot and pretend there is no problem", which is Rand's attitude.

I shudder at defending Objectivism, but this has gotten far too
distorted for my taste.

It is not a fact that my senses might have nothing to do with reality. 
I will state unequivocally that my senses have to do with reality. I
may make mistakes in my conclusions from that sense data, but the data
is real. The sensations are real. I might conclude from what I see
that something was very big, only to find that close up, it was not.
My original view of the scene was not invalid, I merely made a false
conclusion from my sense data.

And while I'm at it, the harping on Existence Exists and A is A makes
a package deal out of two very innocuous statements and other more
dubious claims of Objectivists. Existence Exists, as explained to me
by a longtime Objectivist, simply meant that if you're a brain in a
dish, then you're a brain in a dish. It is a cry for consistency in
thought. You will not be a goat and not a goat at the same time and in
the same sense of the word. You will be something n particular, not
everything at once. Not too objectionable in my book.

